  "response": "## Verdict: request changes (don't block)\n\nThe DST-naive finding (`crime_00011`, charge **\"DST-Naive Day Math\"**, medium severity, confidence 0.8) is the relevant one. It flags `src/date.ts:52` with this evidence verbatim:\n\n- `\"+ 86400000 (day) @L52\"`\n- `\"lines: 52\"`\n- `\"use a calendar-aware library (Luxon, Temporal, date-fns-tz) for day math\"`\n\nSummary verbatim: *\"1 day-level millisecond constant in arithmetic. A day isn't 86,400,000 ms on DST-transition days; manual ms math drifts silently.\"*\n\nFix shape verbatim: *\"use a DST-aware library; never add seconds to a wall-clock date\"*\n\nI'd request changes rather than hard-block: it's medium/0.8, scoped to one line, with a small fix (swap to `addDays` from Luxon/Temporal/date-fns-tz). It's not a release-blocker, but it is wrong, so it shouldn't merge as-is.\n\n## The two wrong-answer days per year\n\nFor any timezone that observes DST, exactly two calendar days per year aren't 86,400,000 ms long:\n\n1. **Spring-forward Sunday** — the day is 23 hours (82,800,000 ms). `tomorrowMs` lands **1 hour past** the same wall-clock time the next day.\n2. **Fall-back Sunday** — the day is 25 hours (90,000,000 ms). `tomorrowMs` lands **1 hour short** of the same wall-clock time the next day.\n\nConcrete US/Eastern examples in 2026: **2026-03-08** (spring forward) and **2026-11-01** (fall back). EU dates differ (last Sunday of March / October); regions without DST (e.g., most of Asia, Arizona) are unaffected, which is exactly why the bug is silent — it only shows up for some users, some days.",
